<title>USA: High Plains Wheat - Dealers take protection.</title>
<headline>High Plains Wheat - Dealers take protection.</headline>
<dateline>KANSAS CITY, Mo. 1997-07-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Grain dealers were taking two to five cents per bushel protection on hard red winter wheat Friday as KCBT wheat futures were called to open lower on bearish data in USDA's July crop report.</p>
<p>USDA pegged the 1997 U.S. winter wheat crop at 1.781 billion bushels, which exceeded the average analysts' expectation of 1.687 million bushels.</p>
<p>Dealers said farmers were still selling wheat Friday morning despite the protection.</p>
<p>&quot;Even with two cents protection we're still buying wheat,&quot; an Oklahoma dealer said.</p>
<p>Rains fell across Kansas and Oklahoma Thursday night and more rain was expected to hit the area Friday. Dealers said this could slow efforts to wrap up harvesting.  </p>
